|---|---|---|---|
| **HIGH (defect)** | plan.md:51, 60 | Re-emission idempotency is claimed via "unique identities", but `dao.space.index` in `:unresolved` mode allocates a fresh durable id for every tempid in every batch (`idx.cljc:891-907`, `:942-980`) and never reads `:db/unique`; the only unique-identity resolver in the codebase is the transactor (`transact.cljc:302-319`). A throwing `load` re-reads the batch (`observer.cljc:236-238`), so a retried encoder mints a second occurrence entity with a distinct id. The mechanism the plan names does not exist on the path the plan chose. | Either route side-table datoms through a transactor (finding 2's shape, which also gives unique-identity upsert), or drop the unique-identity claim and make emission idempotent by construction: stage the outputs and resume with a `:next` pointer, as `attempt-publication` does (`idx.cljc:1064-1079`) and as cat.md §8.4.1 rules ("a staged retry reuses the identity"). |
| **HIGH (defect)** | plan.md:24, 26-27, 35, 60 | S2 has two writers (encoder, VM lowering) appending bare datoms. `admitting-datom?` requires each row to carry an integer `t ≥ 0` (`idx.cljc:862-863`), and the index never mints `t` (`dao.space.index.as-observer.md:259-261`, decision 4). So each writer chooses `t`, violating cat.md §8.1: "`t` is never chosen by the writer" and `t` is "the datom `t` the transactor stamped" (cat.md:1271-1283). §8.6 as-of and current-name resolution fold on `t` (cat.md:1679-1706); with two independent clocks or all-zero `t`, as-of is meaningless and a retract-then-reassert of `:yin/code` becomes "conflicting history" (as-observer.md:264-270). The as-observer doc says this itself: a composition that wants transaction-time semantics routes the medium through a transactor. | Make S2 a request medium: writers append tempid datoms with `t` nil, as cat.md:1292-1294 defines transactor input. One transactor process observes S2, stamps `t`, resolves tempids and unique identities, and appends `{:dao.space/transaction {:t :datoms}}` records to a ledger medium S3. The index session observes S3 in `:resolved` mode. This is single-writer (T6, `dao.space.transactor.md:75`), not the double-write the v1 review rejected, because nothing on S2 is stamped before the transactor sees it. If the plan instead wants no transactor, it must declare S2 assertion-only with `t 0`, give up §8.6 on it, and say so. |
| **HIGH (defect, new)** | plan.md:14-18, 43-44, 49 | The pre-boundary Map AST is a public medium with three consumers, two of which consume non-canonical code. cat.md:158-162: the map AST is "never hashed directly, never stored, never shipped"; cat.md:360: De Bruijn resolution "happens in the frontend before the map AST is emitted"; §9.1:1749-1754: the frontend owns the boundary projection. The plan moves resolution into the encoder (plan.md:49), so the Map AST topic carries named variables, `:yang/*` keys, and `:macro?` flags. The AST Walker "fast path" (plan.md:43-44) executes that directly, yet §9.1:1731 changes the walker's `:variable` arm to consume indices, so the fast path runs a program the migrated walker cannot evaluate. It also bypasses the §7.4 validator, the §7.2 "both paths, one validator" obligation (cat.md:937-939), and the §8.5 stray-macro-lambda check (cat.md:1650-1657). The "Map AST Indexer → dao.space" stage (plan.md:17-18) indexes a map AST into `dao.space`, which indexes datoms only (cat.md:777, 892-894, 1805-1806), and would be a fourth code representation §1 excludes (cat.md:75-80). | Perform the whole boundary projection (strip, De Bruijn, saturate, positionalize, merkle, envelope) before anything observable is emitted, either inside the frontend per §9.1 or in the encoder with the frontend→encoder link composition-private. The walker observes S1 exactly as the VM does and loads `rows → map` (cat.md:912-914). Delete the Map AST Indexer; the `$ast` relation and the §6.5 datom projection are the query surfaces. |
